a }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $10:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 5,\\pm 10\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-13(1)+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-13$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442273 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3399fe8e-8176-5e35-93d5-9a34329ea9c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442273 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186272116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442274 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186442277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442274 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34fc937b-c4ca-51d0-b41f-cc48a4e031b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442274 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186442271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442275 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186442273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442275 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41fe8de5-4b38-5378-88a7-29da8122c672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442275 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186442272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442276 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186442274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442276 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4407ab4d-760d-5929-a4aa-ceca8bb19594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1